Server-side tests for CIVS
==========================

These cover the Perl half of CIVS: the CGI scripts and the modules behind
them. A throwaway installation is built under tests/server/work and
exercised there, so nothing outside that directory is touched, no mail is
sent, and no existing poll data is read or written.

    tests/server/run-tests                  build from config/sample
    tests/server/run-tests <settings-file>  build from another settings file
    tests/server/run-tests -k               keep the installation to poke at

The base settings file only supplies the things that do not matter here;
the install paths, LOCALDEBUG and ADMIN_KEY are overridden by the runner.
LOCALDEBUG makes create_election print the control URL rather than mailing
it, which is how the tests learn the id of a poll they have just created.

The suites
----------

compile   Runs perl -c over every module and script in the installation,
          which is the only way to see the scripts compile with their
          @PLACEHOLDER@ settings substituted. Two things make this less
          simple than it looks, both explained in run-tests: election.pm
          exits the process when it is loaded without a poll to work on,
          and CGI::Carp turns a compilation failure into a web page and a
          zero exit status. Between them they once let this suite pass a
          script that was garbage below its "use election" line. The offline tools --
          tabulate, dump_elections and election_utils.pm -- are not
          installed by install-civs, so they are checked in cgi-bin where
          they are run from.

storage   Creates polls through create_election and reads them back
          through election.pm, checking how questions are laid out in the
          database: that a poll asking one question stores exactly what it
          always did, that further questions are stored under "qN." keys,
          that a poll written before multiple questions existed still
          loads as a one-question poll, and that election_utils.pm, which
          the offline tools read through, agrees with election.pm.

legacy    A poll created before polls could ask more than one question,
          with ballots already cast. The fixture is a poll made the
          ordinary way and then stripped of every key that only exists
          now, which is what such a poll looks like on disk. It has to
          go on tabulating, downloading, accepting votes and accepting
          write-ins with no sign that anything changed under it.

control   The supervisor's page for a poll that asks several questions:
          that each question's choices, text and winner count are shown
          under their own heading, that a write-in is added to and removed
          from the question it belongs to, that disallowing write-ins
          turns them off for every question, that the ballots answering
          each question are counted separately, and that a poll asking one
          question looks as it always has.

results   Tabulating a poll that asks several questions: that each is
          reported separately from its own ballots, with its own settings
          form and its own cache file, that the poll's own description
          and the advertisement for other polls appear once rather than
          per question, that a question a voter skipped leaves them out
          of its tally, and that a poll asking one question produces what
          it always did, from a cache file of the same name.

voting    Casting a ballot on a poll that asks several questions: that
          each question is recorded as it is answered rather than at the
          end, that the receipt leads back into a ballot in progress and
          can change an answer already given without counting it twice,
          that skipping records nothing and withdraws anything recorded
          before, that a voter returning without their receipt is turned
          away, and that a poll asking one question behaves exactly as it
          always has.

ballots   Downloading and uploading ballots: that each question's are
          handled on their own, in the format a poll asking one question
          has always used, that a question a voter skipped contributes no
          row, and that uploading a test poll's ballots for one question
          leaves the others alone.

Writing a test
--------------

Write tests/server/test-<name>.pl taking the installation directory and
the source directory as its two arguments, exiting non-zero on failure,
then add a block for it in run-tests. To drive a CGI script, POST to it
the way test-storage.pl does: the request body has to be a real file
rather than an in-memory handle, since it must survive the exec.

Give ck the prototype the existing suites use. Without it a failing match
yields an empty list rather than a false value, the arguments shift along,
and the text meant for the failure message arrives as the condition -- so
the check passes exactly when it should not.
